---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import GameCard from '../../components/GameCard.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
import games from '../../data/games.json';
import soon from '../../data/coming-soon.json';
import { url } from '../../config';

const math = games.filter(g => g.category === 'math-games');
const jsonLd = [{
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList', itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: new URL(url(), Astro.site).href },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Math games', item: new URL(url('math-games/'), Astro.site).href },
  ],
}];
---
<Base
  title="Free Math Games for Kids, Pre-K to Grade 2 | EduLearnLabs"
  description="Free math games for kids ages 4 to 8: counting, adding within 20, shapes and more. Short, kind, ad-free practice that adapts to your child."
  path="math-games/" nav="math" jsonLd={jsonLd}>

  <Breadcrumbs back={{ name: 'Home', path: '' }} items={[{ name: 'Home', path: '' }, { name: 'Math games', path: 'math-games/' }]} />

  <h1>Free math games for kids</h1>
  <p class="lead prose" style="font-size:20px">Short math games for Pre-K to Grade 2. Each one practices a single skill,
    takes about 2 to 5 minutes and ends with a happy win. Pick a game and press Play.</p>

  <div class="grid">
    {math.map(g => <GameCard game={g} />)}
  </div>

  <h2>Coming soon</h2>
  <ul class="soon-list">
    {soon['math-games'].map(g => <li>{g.title} <span class="muted">({g.grade})</span></li>)}
  </ul>

  <AdSlot id="math-mid" />

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>What kids learn, grade by grade</h2>
    <h3>Pre-K (ages 4 to 5)</h3>
    <p>Counting objects up to 10, matching each object to one number word, and naming simple shapes like circles,
      squares and triangles. At this age, math is about noticing how many and what shape. Counting things your child
      can see and touch builds the base for everything after.</p>
    <h3>Kindergarten (ages 5 to 6)</h3>
    <p>Counting to 20, comparing which group has more, and first addition with objects: three apples and two apples
      make five apples. Kids learn that adding means putting groups together.</p>
    <h3>Grade 1 (ages 6 to 7)</h3>
    <p>Adding and subtracting within 20, and being quick and sure within 10. This is where
      <a href={url('math-games/addition-for-grade-1/')}>Addition for Grade 1</a> fits: children fill baskets for garden friends,
      move from small sums to sums that cross ten, like 8 + 7, and practice the facts they found tricky last time.</p>
    <h3>Grade 2 (ages 7 to 8)</h3>
    <p>Adding and subtracting within 100, skip counting by 2s, 5s and 10s, and understanding tens and ones.
      Skip counting is the first step toward times tables.</p>

    <h2>How our math games work</h2>
    <p><strong>One skill, one game.</strong> A game about adding only asks about adding. Clear practice helps a child
      feel progress, and it helps you see what they can do.</p>
    <p><strong>Hands before numbers.</strong> Children move real-looking objects, like flowers into a basket or stones onto
      a ten-frame, so a child who is not sure can count instead of guess.</p>
    <p><strong>Hints, never the answer.</strong> A miss brings a gentle hint, and the child always makes the last tap
      themselves. Nobody gets stuck, and nobody sees a failure screen.</p>
    <p><strong>Just right difficulty.</strong> Three right answers in a row and the game steps up a little. Two misses
      in a row and it steps back. Children spend their time where they learn most.</p>
    <p><strong>Wrong answers that teach us something.</strong> The wrong choices are real mistakes children make,
      such as being one away or subtracting instead of adding. The <a href={url('grown-ups/')}>Grown-ups page</a>
      turns that into a simple tip for you.</p>

    <h2>Tips for parents</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Play one game together first. Say the numbers out loud and count the flowers with your finger.</li>
      <li>Short and often beats long and rare. One game a day is plenty.</li>
      <li>Praise effort: "You counted carefully" helps more than "You are so smart".</li>
      <li>Come back on another day. Skills that stick across days are the ones that last, and that is what
        fills Hazel's garden to a full flower.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Questions parents ask</h2>
    <h3>Are the math games really free?</h3>
    <p>Yes. Every game is free to play, with no ads inside games, no in-game purchases and nothing to unlock with money.</p>
    <h3>Does my child need an account?</h3>
    <p>No. There is no login and no sign up. Progress is saved only in this browser on this device, and you can
      clear it any time on the Grown-ups page.</p>
    <h3>Which devices work?</h3>
    <p>Any modern browser: tablets, phones, laptops and Chromebooks. The games are small and load in a few seconds,
      even on older school computers and slow connections.</p>
    <h3>Can brothers and sisters share one device?</h3>
    <p>Yes. Each child picks an animal, like a fox or a panda, and their stars and progress stay separate.
      No names are needed.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
